  "preamble": "The diphthong [oi] becomes [ai] when its mid back rounded nucleus unrounds and lowers to [a]. The well-attested cases are side effects of branch-wide mergers of PIE *o with *a: in Proto-Germanic (PIE *h₁óynos 'one' > *ainaz > Gothic ains), in Balto-Slavic (*h₁óynos > *aiˀnas, Old Prussian ains; *kʷoynéh₂ 'price' > *kainā́ˀ), and in Indo-Iranian, where *e, *o and *a all merged as *a (*óywos 'one' > *Háywas > Old Persian aivaʰ).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Unrounding, Lowering, Merger",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Proto-Germanic",
      "languageFamily": "Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "PIE *h₁óynos 'one'",
          "to": "Proto-Germanic *ainaz > Gothic ains",
          "note": "Part of the Proto-Germanic merger of PIE *o and *a; the diphthong *oy fell together with *ay."
        },
        {
          "from": "PIE *wóyde 'knows' (perfect of *weyd- 'see')",
          "to": "Proto-Germanic *wait > Gothic wait 'I/he know(s)'",
          "note": "The o-grade perfect diphthong surfaces as *ai in the Germanic preterite-present verb *witaną."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Proto-Balto-Slavic",
      "languageFamily": "Balto-Slavic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "PIE *h₁óynos 'one'",
          "to": "Proto-Balto-Slavic *aiˀnas > Old Prussian ains 'one'",
          "note": "Balto-Slavic also merged PIE *o with *a, so *oy became *ai; Lithuanian vienas continues the same form with a prothetic v- and later ai > ie."
        },
        {
          "from": "PIE *kʷoynéh₂ 'price, penalty'",
          "to": "Proto-Balto-Slavic *kainā́ˀ > Proto-Slavic *cěna 'price'",
          "note": "Slavic later monophthongized *ai to *ě; Lithuanian kaina keeps the diphthong."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Proto-Indo-Iranian",
      "languageFamily": "Indo-Iranian",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "PIE *óywos 'one'",
          "to": "Proto-Indo-Iranian *Háywas > Old Persian aivaʰ, Avestan aēuua",
          "note": "Indo-Iranian merged PIE *e, *o, *a as *a, so *oy fell together with *ay; Old Persian keeps the diphthong ai."
        },
        {
          "from": "PIE *h₁óy-kos 'one'",
          "to": "Proto-Indo-Iranian *Háykas",
          "note": "Continued in Indo-Aryan (Sanskrit eka, after the later monophthongization *ai > e)."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
